if you were asked to think of notable  crusaders likely candidates would  include richard the lionheart raymond of  toulouse or frederick barbarossa very  few would ever think of King Sigurd the  first of Norway who was in fact the  first King to personally go gallivanting  off to the Holy Land Norway which was  currently going through a period of  independence from Denmark had converted  to Christianity in the late 10th century  by the time of sick at the firsts reign  which he shared with his brother ace T  in the kingdom had been Christian for  over a century the Scandinavian world  had a pretty poor relationship with Rome  but the first monarchs to personally  take up the Pope's call to arms was of  course Sigurd you should be aware though  that there is still some debate as to  whether or not this was a crusade or a  pilgrimage Sigurd left Norway 1107 to  begin his journey to Jerusalem and  stopped off in England in order to  winter there Sigurd left early next year  without burning the country down a  conquering it which was a nice change of  pace and had itself to Iberia and Iberia  was at this point divided between the  Christian realms to the north in the  numerous Islamic ones to the South  Sigrid's fleet sailed to northern iberia  and took refuge in Yakov's land I've  always known as Santiago the lord of the  city allowed sigur to remain and  purchase whatever food he needed until  the winter came when he changed his mind  as such he had agreed to leave  peacefully and just kidding he sacked  the city and stole all of the food  anyway they passed no verson nowadays  called Gibraltar are made their way to  the Balearic Islands and it was here  that one of the most important parts of  the Norwegian Crusade occurred you see  the balearic islands were also Muslim  held but geographically important too  they were for the most part safe havens  for pirates but were also major centres  in the Mediterranean slave trade they  had long been a thorn in the side of  